35,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"Transport costs vary wildly depending on distance, and whole-building lets carry a high financial risk if people cancel late. We should be very cautious about choosing a distant or private venue.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Shion,,"Staying on campus is impractical; the hall is overcrowded, forcing us into awkward training hours. Also, inland public centers seem to have restrictive rules and cramped sleeping conditions that would likely hinder our recovery.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Minato,,"I'm worried about the workload. Public bookings involve a huge, stressful application process, and staying on campus means someone has to spend hours every day cooking because the canteen is closed. We should avoid those.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"Public beds are cheap, but attendance is a real issue. A fifth of the members didn't even show up to away camps last year, and many of them left the club shortly after. We can't ignore that.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"If we stay on campus, a third of us can't even make it by 9 AM without paying for a bed. Plus, the seniors say we only actually bond when we all sleep in the same place.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Minato, if public bookings are that stressful and campus requires too much labor, we are in a bind. However, @Misaki's point about attendance makes a distant, expensive trip even riskier.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Minato"",""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Asuka, exactly. If we want quality training, we can't have members exhausted by poor sleep or erratic schedules. The campus and inland options both seem to fail the basic requirement of a functional environment.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Minato,,"@Shion, I agree. If we want everyone to be happy and not exhausted, we need a place that is easy to manage. Maybe a place with its own staff would be best for everyone?","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Minato, a place with staff would certainly reduce the burden on organizers. However, we must be careful. If we choose a private venue and people don't show up, the financial gap could be significant.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Misaki, I get the money worry, but if we don't go somewhere where we all stay together, we're just wasting our time. We need a place that actually makes us a team, not just a list of names.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Takumi, I understand the need for bonding, but we cannot ignore the financial risk. If we choose a private lodge and people drop out, the remaining members will have to cover the cost. That is unfair.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Shion,,"@Asuka, while the risk is there, the alternatives are worse. Campus is a logistical mess and inland centers are essentially barracks. If we prioritize training quality and recovery, we need a venue that actually functions.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Minato,,"@Asuka, @Takumi, I really don't want anyone to be stressed or burdened. If a private lodge has staff to handle the meals and bookings, maybe the organizers can actually focus on training and resting too?","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Minato, that's true, but we must be realistic. If we go with a private lodge, we need a way to ensure the costs are covered regardless of attendance. I can't support a plan that leaves the club account in deficit.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Misaki, we can't just play it safe and let the club fall apart. If people stop coming because they don't feel connected, the account won't matter anyway. We need a place that works for everyone.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Misaki, @Takumi, we are balancing financial stability against social cohesion. If we avoid the high transport costs of a distant lodge and the risks of non-refundable deposits, we might find a middle ground. Let's see the options.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Misaki"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Shion,,"@Asuka, a middle ground is only useful if it doesn't compromise the training. If the options are as restrictive as the data suggests, we must choose the one that ensures the best performance and recovery.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Minato,,"I just want us to pick something where no one feels overwhelmed. If we can find a place that handles the hard work for us, everyone will be much happier.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Misaki,,"I agree that we need a sustainable plan. If we can find an option that balances a manageable workload for organizers with a stable cost structure, that would be the most logical choice.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"Whatever the options are, let's not settle for something that keeps us divided. If we can find a way to stay together without breaking the bank, that's the only way this camp actually succeeds.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
